WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.180
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.630
When we show a photograph to someone, most
of the time we are interested in sharing our

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:09.630
memories.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:14.570
Graduation, family festivities beautiful landscapes
are common examples of this.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:18.970
With the recent ascendancy of these amazing
neural style transfer techniques, we can take

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:25.010
a painting, or any other source image, and
transfer the style of this image to our contents.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:28.780
The style is transfered, but the contents
remains unchanged.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:33.430
This takes place by running the images through
a deep neural network, which, in its deeper

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.960
layers, learns about high level concepts such
as artistic style.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.390
This work has sparked a large body of followup
research works.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.820
Feedforward real-time style transfer, temporally
coherent style transfer for videos, you name

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:47.820
it.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.060
However, these techniques are always about
taking one image for content, and one for

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.060
style.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:57.890
How about a new problem formulation where
we paste in a part of a foreign image with

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.520
a completely different style?

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.790
For instance, if you feel that this ancient
artwork is sorely missing a Captain America

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:10.720
shield, or if Picasso's self-portrait is just
not cool enough without shades, then this

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.170
algorithm is for you.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:17.180
However, if we just drop in this part of a
foreign image, anyone can immediately tell

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.050
because of the differences in color and style.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:25.090
A previous, non-AI-based technique does way
better, but it is still apparent that the

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.880
image has been tampered with.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.869
But as you can see here, this new technique
is able to do it seamlessly.

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:36.360
It works by first performing style transfer
from the painting to the new region, and then,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.800
in the second step, additional refinements
are made to it to make sure that the response

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.570
of our neural network is similar across the
entirety of the painting.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:49.960
It is conjectured that if the neural network
is stimulated the same way by every part of

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.049
the image, then there shouldn't be outlier
regions that look vastly different.

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:57.130
And as you can see here, it works remarkably
well on a range of inputs.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.130
I hope these scroll animations come out really
smooth and creamy.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.130
This video took a long time to render in 4K
resolution with 60 frames per second, and

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.130
was only possible because of your support
on Patreon.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:01.130
If you wish to help us create better videos
in the future, please click the Patreon link

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:02.130
in the video description and support the series.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.130
To validate this work, a user study was done
that revealed that the users preferred the

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.560
new technique over the older ones in 15 out
16 images.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.629
I think it is fair to say that this work smokes
the competition.

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:12.939
But what about comparisons to real paintings?

00:02:12.939 --> 00:02:16.569
A different user study was also created to
answer this question.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:21.829
And the answer is that users were mostly unable
to identify whether the painting was tampered

00:02:21.829 --> 00:02:22.829
with.

00:02:22.829 --> 00:02:23.829
Excellent work.

00:02:23.829 --> 00:02:25.719
The source code is also available, so let
the experiments begin!

00:02:25.719 --> 00:02:29.090
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

